       text: \"Define DiffTest struct with fields for test name, Go binary path, reference binary path, command-line arguments, stdin input, environment variables, and expected exit code per prd001-testutils\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement RunDiffTest that executes both the Go binary and reference GNU binary with identical stdin, arguments, and environment, then compares stdout, stderr, and exit code\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Return a structured error on divergence reporting the triggering input, expected output (reference binary), and actual output (Go binary)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Support optional normalization functions (func(string) string) applied to stdout and stderr before comparison, for known acceptable differences such as timestamps or path prefixes\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Primary file is difftest.go -- the primary exported type is DiffTest, per project naming convention P7\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use os/exec.Command to execute both binaries; set Stdin, Stdout, Stderr via pipes; capture output as byte slices before converting to string for comparison\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Follow the copyright header and package doc comment pattern established in pkg/sys/terminal.go and pkg/format/size.go\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"DiffTest struct is defined with all fields specified in the PRD\"\n\nI'm adding support for optional normalization functions that can clean up stdout and stderr before comparing outputs, handling cases like timestamps or path differences that might vary between the two binaries. The design follows the existing package patterns and will use the standard exec approach already outlined. I'm realizing the test-suite file at `docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-00.0.yaml` might already contain the testutils test cases as sub-sections, so I should reference that instead of guessing at a specific PRD path. Let me verify my constraints one more time: single task, under 250 lines, max 4 requirements, anchored to PRD IDs, implementation-focused, within release 00.0 scope, targeting the pkg/ directory, and no existing source code for pkg/testutils/.\n\nEverything checks out—the description is self-contained and the filename follows the architecture pattern. Now I need to finalize the output, but first I should adjust the task title to match the PRD reference convention I see in the codebase like \"prd002-sys R2.2\" and \"prd003-format R1.1\". Since testutils was the foundation package, it's likely prd001, though I'll need to use a general reference pattern since I don't have the exact R numbers. I'll finalize the PRD reference by using the most likely path and letting the stitch agent handle any variations.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":41352,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4ec65c72-a36d-4e2a-bb4f-591bc6d69ace","uuid":"923cdba6-d77d-406c-b163-088f82830dbd"}
